2026-10-04T09:02:01.7275200Z     holds      no merge code path exists; the module's public surface is an allow-list
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7276188Z     fails when the twin is composed with a shell tool, an MCP GitHub server or a subagent with `gh` — the absence still holds and the guarantee is gone, with no diff to twin/ at all
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7277034Z   layer      tool-call boundary   twin/enact_guard.py, registered as a PreToolUse hook
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7277583Z     holds      a disposing tool call is refused before it runs, including a subagent's own
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7278558Z     fails when the registration is deleted, or the twin is driven by a runner that does not honour hooks — a policy check is a call site, and a forgotten call site fails open in silence
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7279918Z   dependency 17 cross-repository pin(s) (3 self-sync, counted separately): driftwood-composed, feeds, ico, insurer, ludlow-composed, nist, platform, tuppence-composed consumed by driftwood, insurer, ludlow, tuppence
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7281378Z     limit      0 of 20 pins name a tag and no commit, so the pin is only as immutable as the tag. Every commit line in this estate is a commented-out placeholder; until one is filled in, 'pinned' means 'pinned to a movable name'.
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7282997Z     limit      3 of 20 pins are a repository syncing itself rather than consuming anybody's policy, and are counted separately: only the 17 cross-repository pins evidence a dependency at all.
2026-10-04T09:02:01.7284864Z     limit      the signature is the sources' own declaration, checked by nothing here: no tag is verified, no Rekor entry is looked up, and estate/verify/provenance/verify-provenance.sh records that this repository's own commits are not keyless-signed either. 'Signed' is therefore a property of the design, not an observation, and the word does no work until something verifies it.
2026-10-04T09:02:10.2995751Z     limit      the consumer repositories are separate by URL and not yet by existence: estate/README.md describes a monorepo-style working tree whose top-level directories become their own GitHub repositories *at split*, and each up.sh rewrites the pinned URL to an in-cluster git server for the offline demo. The pins name real separate repositories; whether those repositories are live is a question this code does not ask.
2026-10-04T09:02:10.3000768Z     limit      read from committed sources, not from a running cluster: this evidences what the repositories declare they consume, never that a control is in force right now. Whether continuous proof of force is required at all is build ticket 65's question and is still open.
2026-10-04T09:02:10.3004786Z     limit      these pins are read from this tool's own repository (.estate-clone), not from any repository 'netflix' owns. The dependency block above evidences what THIS estate consumes; it names no pin, tag or repository belonging to the subject the proposal is about, and 'netflix' is not assumed to have an estate under Flux at all.
2026-10-04T09:02:10.3009706Z   narrowed   Policy-as-code is AN enactment arm, not THE definition of governance. Responses are priced by the FAIR factor they modify and most levers are not code, so if versioned policy were the shape of governance the cross-domain comparison the £ engine exists for could not exist.
